<section xml:id="section-polar-coordinates" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Polar Coordinates and Graphing Polar Equations</title>

<p>
Usually we use Cartesian coordinates, which are directed distances from
two perpendicular axes. Here we will study a new coordinate system,
called the <term>polar coordinate system</term>, which is more
convenient for many purposes such as finding the volume of some
objects.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-definition">
<title>The Polar Coordinate System</title>

<p>
Similar to the Cartesian coordinate system, we need two real numbers to
specify the location of a point <m>P</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane. The
first one is the directed distance from the origin and it is shown by
<m>r</m> in <xref ref="fig-polar-point-prefigure"/>. The second one is
the directed angle <m>\theta</m>, and an angle is positive if measured
in the counterclockwise direction from the positive <m>x</m>-axis and
negative in the clockwise direction. The ordered pair <m>(r,\theta)</m> represents
the location of a point <m>P</m> in the polar coordinate system.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-point-prefigure">
<caption>
The same point <m>P</m> in both coordinate systems: in Cartesian
coordinates it is <m>P(x,y)</m>, and in polar coordinates it is
<m>P(r,\theta)</m>, where <m>r</m> is the directed distance from the
origin <m>O</m> and <m>\theta</m> is the directed angle measured
counterclockwise from the positive <m>x</m>-axis. The two are related
by <m>x = r\cos\theta</m> and <m>y = r\sin\theta</m>.
</caption>
<image width="65%">
<shortdescription>
A point P labeled in both Cartesian and polar coordinates, with r and theta marked.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-polar-point">
<diagram dimensions="(360,221)" margins="10">
<definition>arc(t) = (0.55*cos(t), 0.55*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.4,3.9,2.2)">
<axes at="pp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<line at="pp-ray" endpoints="((0,0),(2.6,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
    thickness="3"/>
<line at="pp-yleg" endpoints="((2.6,0),(2.6,1.5))" stroke="#E69F00"
    thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<parametric-curve at="pp-arc" function="arc" domain="(0,0.523)"
    stroke="#CC79A7" thickness="2"/>
<point at="pp-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>O</m>
</point>
<point at="pp-P" p="(2.6,1.5)" fill="#0072B2" alignment="n">
<m>P(x,y) = P(r,\theta)</m>
</point>
<point at="pp-xfoot" p="(2.6,0)" fill="#E69F00" alignment="s">
<m>x = r\cos\theta</m>
</point>
<label at="pp-yleg-label" anchor="(2.68,0.75)" alignment="e"
    color="#E69F00"><m>y = r\sin\theta</m></label>
<label at="pp-r" anchor="(1.25,0.9)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>r</m>
</label>
<label at="pp-theta" anchor="(0.68,0.22)" alignment="e"
    color="#CC79A7"><m>\theta</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Cartesian x and y axes with a point P in the first quadrant labeled both P of x and y and P of r and theta. A blue segment of length r runs from the origin O to P, a small reddish purple arc marks the directed angle theta at the origin, and a dashed orange vertical segment of length y equals r sine theta drops from P to the point x equals r cosine theta on the x axis.">
  <annotation ref="pp-ray" text="The segment of directed length r from the origin O to the point P."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-arc" text="The directed angle theta, measured counterclockwise from the positive x axis."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-yleg" text="The dashed vertical leg of length y equals r sine theta."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-xfoot" text="The foot of the vertical leg, at x equals r cosine theta on the x axis."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-P" text="The point P, with Cartesian coordinates x comma y and polar coordinates r comma theta."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-polar-negative-r-prefigure"/> demonstrates the meaning
of negative <m>r</m>. Note that the polar pair <m>(-r,\theta)</m> is
the same as <m>(r,\theta+\pi)</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-negative-r-prefigure">
<caption>
The meaning of negative <m>r</m>: the point <m>(-r,\theta)</m> lies on
the ray opposite to the ray of angle <m>\theta</m>, so
<m>(-r,\theta)</m> is the same point as <m>(r,\theta+\pi)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
A ray at angle theta and its opposite ray, showing a point with negative r.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-polar-negative-r">
<diagram dimensions="(360,228)" margins="10">
<definition>arc1(t) = (0.5*cos(t), 0.5*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>arc2(t) = (0.72*cos(t), 0.72*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.0,-1.7,2.9,1.9)">
<line at="nr-axis" endpoints="((0,0),(2.6,0))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2"/>
<vector at="nr-axis-arrow" v="(0.15,0)" tail="(2.55,0)" stroke="black"
    thickness="2"/>
<line at="nr-ray" endpoints="((0,0),(2.2,1.35))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2"/>
<line at="nr-opposite" endpoints="((0,0),(-1.9,-1.17))"
    stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<parametric-curve at="nr-arc1" function="arc1" domain="(0,0.55)"
    stroke="#CC79A7" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="nr-arc2" function="arc2" domain="(0,3.69)"
    stroke="#CC79A7" thickness="2"/>
<point at="nr-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="se">
<m>O</m>
</point>
<point at="nr-P" p="(2.2,1.35)" fill="#0072B2" alignment="ne">
<m>(r,\theta)</m>
</point>
<point at="nr-negP" p="(-1.9,-1.17)" fill="#0072B2" alignment="sw">
<m>(-r,\theta)</m>
</point>
<label at="nr-theta" anchor="(0.62,0.18)" alignment="e"
    color="#CC79A7"><m>\theta</m></label>
<label at="nr-thetapi" anchor="(-0.55,0.75)" alignment="nw"
    color="#CC79A7"><m>\theta+\pi</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A ray from the origin at angle theta with the point r comma theta marked on it, and the dashed opposite ray at angle theta plus pi with the point negative r comma theta marked on it. Two reddish purple arcs mark the angles theta and theta plus pi.">
  <annotation ref="nr-ray" text="The ray at angle theta, containing the point with positive first coordinate r."/>
  <annotation ref="nr-opposite" text="The dashed opposite ray, at angle theta plus pi. The point negative r comma theta lies on this ray, at distance r from the origin."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>



</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-cartesian-relation">
<title>Relation Between Polar and Cartesian Coordinates</title>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-cartesian-prefigure">
<caption>
The common origin and initial ray of the polar and Cartesian systems.
For a point <m>P(x,y) = P(r,\theta)</m>, the right triangle with legs
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m> and hypotenuse <m>r</m> relates the two
coordinate systems.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
A point P on a circle labeled in both coordinate systems, with the right triangle of x, y, and r.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-polar-cartesian">
<diagram dimensions="(360,327)" margins="10">
<definition>arc(t) = (0.5*cos(t), 0.5*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>unitcircle(t) = (1.7*cos(t), 1.7*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.3,-2.2,2.9,2.5)">
<axes at="pc-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="pc-circle" function="unitcircle"
    domain="(0,2*pi)" stroke="#bbbbbb" thickness="1"/>
<line at="pc-r" endpoints="((0,0),(1.45,0.89))" stroke="#CC79A7"
    thickness="3"/>
<line at="pc-x" endpoints="((0,0),(1.45,0))" stroke="#E69F00"
    thickness="3"/>
<line at="pc-y" endpoints="((1.45,0),(1.45,0.89))" stroke="#E69F00"
    thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="pc-arc" function="arc" domain="(0,0.55)"
    stroke="#CC79A7" thickness="2"/>
<point at="pc-P" p="(1.45,0.89)" fill="#0072B2" alignment="n">
<m>P(x,y) = P(r,\theta)</m>
</point>
<label at="pc-r-label" anchor="(0.6,0.52)" alignment="nw"
    color="#CC79A7"><m>r</m></label>
<label at="pc-x-label" anchor="(0.75,-0.08)" alignment="s"
    color="#E69F00"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="pc-y-label" anchor="(1.53,0.42)" alignment="e"
    color="#E69F00"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="pc-theta" anchor="(0.62,0.16)" alignment="e"
    color="#CC79A7"><m>\theta</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Cartesian axes with a gray circle centered at the common origin of the two coordinate systems. A point P in the first quadrant is labeled both P of x and y and P of r and theta. A reddish purple segment of length r joins the origin to P, and orange segments of lengths x and y form the legs of the right triangle. A small arc marks the angle theta at the origin.">
  <annotation ref="pc-r" text="The hypotenuse of the right triangle: the polar distance r from the common origin to P."/>
  <annotation ref="pc-x" text="The horizontal leg: the Cartesian coordinate x, equal to r cosine theta."/>
  <annotation ref="pc-y" text="The vertical leg: the Cartesian coordinate y, equal to r sine theta."/>
  <annotation ref="pc-arc" text="The angle theta between the initial ray and the segment from the origin to P."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
From the right triangle in <xref ref="fig-polar-cartesian-prefigure"/>,
write <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in terms of <m>(r,\theta)</m>, and then
<m>r</m> and <m>\theta</m> in terms of <m>(x,y)</m>. (Given
<m>(r,\theta)</m> the point <m>(x,y)</m> is unique; given
<m>(x,y) \ne (0,0)</m>, how many choices are there for <m>r</m>, and
how many for <m>\theta \in [0,2\pi)</m> once <m>r</m> is chosen?)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$x =$ \qquad $y =$ \qquad $r^2 =$ \qquad $\tan\theta =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {given $(x,y)$: choices for $r$, and for $\theta$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the four relations between polar and Cartesian coordinates and one for the discussion of how many polar pairs a given Cartesian point has.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-polar-cartesian-equations">
<title>Equations Relating Polar and Cartesian Coordinates</title>
<p>
<men xml:id="eq-polar-cartesian">
x = r\cos\theta, \qquad
y = r\sin\theta, \qquad
r^2 = x^2 + y^2, \qquad
\tan\theta = \frac{y}{x}
</men>
</p>
</assemblage>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-nonuniqueness">
<title>Non-Uniqueness of Polar Coordinates</title>

<p>
Unlike Cartesian coordinates, polar coordinates are not unique: a point
has many representations. For example, in
<xref ref="fig-polar-representations-prefigure"/> you can see two
different representations, <m>\left(1,\tfrac{13\pi}{4}\right)</m> and
<m>\left(-1,\tfrac{\pi}{4}\right)</m>, of the same point; the pairs
<m>\left(1,\tfrac{5\pi}{4}\right)</m> and
<m>\left(1,-\tfrac{3\pi}{4}\right)</m> locate it as well. In general,
a point denoted by <m>(r,\theta)</m> can also be represented by
<m>(r,\theta + 2n\pi)</m> or <m>(-r,\theta + (2n+1)\pi)</m>, where
<m>n</m> is any integer, and all the representations of a point can be
generated this way from any one of them.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-representations-prefigure">
<caption>
Two of the many polar representations of the same point. On the left,
<m>\left(1,\tfrac{13\pi}{4}\right)</m>: the angle wraps around once
before landing on the third-quadrant ray. On the right,
<m>\left(-1,\tfrac{\pi}{4}\right)</m>: the negative <m>r</m> places
the point on the ray opposite to the angle
<m>\tfrac{\pi}{4}</m>. The pairs <m>\left(1,\tfrac{5\pi}{4}\right)</m>
and <m>\left(1,-\tfrac{3\pi}{4}\right)</m> give the same point too.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
<image>
<shortdescription>
A spiral arc ending at the ray of angle thirteen pi over four.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-polar-rep1">
<diagram dimensions="(280,226)" margins="10">
<definition>arc(t) = ((0.38 + 0.045*t)*cos(t), (0.38 + 0.045*t)*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.9,-1.7,2.4,1.7)">
<line at="rp1-axis" endpoints="((0,0),(2.1,0))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2"/>
<vector at="rp1-arrow" v="(0.15,0)" tail="(2.05,0)" stroke="black"
    thickness="2"/>
<line at="rp1-ray" endpoints="((0,0),(-0.7071,-0.7071))"
    stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="rp1-arc" function="arc" domain="(0,10.21)"
    stroke="#CC79A7" thickness="1.5"/>
<point at="rp1-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="ne">
<m>O</m>
</point>
<point at="rp1-P" p="(-0.7071,-0.7071)" fill="#0072B2" alignment="sw">
<m>\left(1,\tfrac{13\pi}{4}\right)</m>
</point>
<label at="rp1-angle" anchor="(-1.15,0.9)" alignment="nw"
    color="#CC79A7"><m>\tfrac{13\pi}{4}</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The polar axis with a spiral arc winding counterclockwise through more than a full turn, ending at the ray in the third quadrant where the point one comma thirteen pi over four is marked at distance one from the origin.">
  <annotation ref="rp1-arc" text="The angle thirteen pi over four, which winds around more than one full turn before reaching the third quadrant ray."/>
  <annotation ref="rp1-P" text="The point at distance one along the final ray."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<shortdescription>
A ray at angle pi over four with its dashed opposite ray and a negative r point.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-polar-rep2">
<diagram dimensions="(280,226)" margins="10">
<definition>arc(t) = (0.5*cos(t), 0.5*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.9,-1.7,2.4,1.7)">
<line at="rp2-axis" endpoints="((0,0),(2.1,0))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2"/>
<vector at="rp2-arrow" v="(0.15,0)" tail="(2.05,0)" stroke="black"
    thickness="2"/>
<line at="rp2-ray" endpoints="((0,0),(1.06,1.06))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2"/>
<line at="rp2-opposite" endpoints="((0,0),(-0.7071,-0.7071))"
    stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<parametric-curve at="rp2-arc" function="arc" domain="(0,0.785)"
    stroke="#CC79A7" thickness="2"/>
<point at="rp2-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="s">
<m>O</m>
</point>
<point at="rp2-P" p="(-0.7071,-0.7071)" fill="#0072B2" alignment="sw">
<m>\left(-1,\tfrac{\pi}{4}\right)</m>
</point>
<label at="rp2-angle" anchor="(0.62,0.3)" alignment="e"
    color="#CC79A7"><m>\tfrac{\pi}{4}</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The polar axis with a solid ray at angle pi over four in the first quadrant, and its dashed opposite ray in the third quadrant. The point negative one comma pi over four is marked on the dashed opposite ray at distance one from the origin.">
  <annotation ref="rp2-ray" text="The ray at angle pi over four."/>
  <annotation ref="rp2-opposite" text="The dashed opposite ray. Because r equals negative one, the point lies here, at distance one from the origin."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>


<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-to-cartesian-example">
<title>Replacing Polar Equations by Cartesian Equations</title>

<example xml:id="example-polar-to-cartesian">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Replace the following polar equations by equivalent Cartesian
equations.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>r = 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\theta = \pi/4</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>r^2\cos\theta\sin\theta = 4</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>r = 1 + 2r\cos\theta</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Use <xref ref="eq-polar-cartesian"/>. Parts <alert>A</alert> and
<alert>B</alert> are one line each; name the curves. In
<alert>C</alert>, regroup the left side as a product of two familiar
quantities. In <alert>D</alert>, replace <m>r</m> and
<m>r\cos\theta</m>, then square to remove the root.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8.75) -- (15,8.75);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {A. $r = 1$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.45) {B. $\theta = \pi/4$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {C. $r^2\cos\theta\sin\theta = 4$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {D. $r = 1 + 2r\cos\theta$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four labelled rows, one for the Cartesian form of each of the four polar equations.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<!-- The figure fig-polar-to-cartesian-prefigure (the four curves of
     Example I) is hidden at the instructor's request (Oct 2026); it is in
     the completed notes. -->

<p>
Next, we would like to learn about graphing polar equations. We will
begin by analyzing symmetries of polar equations.
</p>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-symmetries">
<title>Symmetries</title>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-symmetry-x-axis">
<title>Symmetry about the <m>x</m>-axis</title>

<p>
Note that a graph is symmetric about the <m>x</m>-axis if for every
point <m>(r,\theta)</m> on the graph, there exists a point with the
coordinates <m>(r,-\theta)</m> on the graph as well. As an example,
consider the equation <m>r = 1 + \cos\theta</m>. Explain why, if
<m>(r,\theta)</m> is a point on the graph of this equation, so is
<m>(r,-\theta)</m>. Then plot its graph: fill in
<xref ref="table-cardioid-points"/>, mark the points on the polar grid
below, use the symmetry to complete the curve, and name it. The process
of sweeping <m>\theta</m> to trace the graph is animated in
<xref ref="fig-cardioid-video"/>; use it to check your sketch.
</p>

<table xml:id="table-cardioid-points">
<title>Some points on the graph of <m>r = 1 + \cos\theta</m></title>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="minor">
<cell><m>\theta</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>\pi/3</m></cell>
<cell><m>\pi/2</m></cell>
<cell><m>2\pi/3</m></cell>
<cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>r = 1+\cos\theta</m></cell>
<cell></cell>
<cell></cell>
<cell></cell>
<cell></cell>
<cell></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>

<sidebyside widths="46% 46%">
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, >=stealth]
\foreach \r in {0.5,1,1.5,2} \draw[gray!35, very thin] (0,0) circle (\r);
\foreach \a in {0,30,...,330} \draw[gray!35, very thin] (0,0) -- (\a:2);
\draw[->, thick] (-2.2,0) -- (2.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2.2) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {1,2} \draw (\x,-0.05) -- (\x,0.05) node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2} \draw (-0.05,\y) -- (0.05,\y) node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty polar grid with circles of radius one half to two and rays every thirty degrees, for plotting the points of the table and sketching the graph of r equals one plus cosine theta.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (7,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (7,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.7) {why $(r,-\theta)$ is also on the graph:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,3.2) {name of the curve:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the symmetry argument and one for the name of the curve.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-video">
<caption>
Animation of graphing the polar equation <m>r = 1 + \cos\theta</m>. A
ray at angle <m>\theta</m> rotates counterclockwise while the distance
<m>r = 1+\cos\theta</m> along the ray is recomputed; the traced point
sweeps out the cardioid, hitting the values of
<xref ref="table-cardioid-points"/> along the way.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-cardioid-trace"
       source="videos/cardioid_trace_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-symmetry-y-axis">
<title>Symmetry about the <m>y</m>-axis</title>

<p>
For every point <m>(r,\theta)</m> on the graph, there exists a point
with the coordinates <m>(-r,-\theta)</m> or <m>(r,\pi-\theta)</m> on
the graph. As an example, consider <m>r = 1 - \sin(\theta)</m>, which
is shown in <xref ref="fig-cardioid-y-prefigure"/>: since
<m>\sin(\pi - \theta) = \sin\theta</m>, the graph is symmetric about
the <m>y</m>-axis. The symmetry is animated in
<xref ref="fig-cardioid-y-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-y-prefigure">
<caption>
The cardioid <m>r = 1 - \sin\theta</m>, symmetric about the
<m>y</m>-axis.
</caption>
<image width="65%">
<shortdescription>
The cardioid r equals one minus sine theta, pointing downward, on a polar grid.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cardioid-y">
<diagram dimensions="(360,321)" margins="10">
<definition>card(t) = ((1-sin(t))*cos(t), (1-sin(t))*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>ring1(t) = (0.5*cos(t), 0.5*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>ring2(t) = (cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
<definition>ring3(t) = (1.5*cos(t), 1.5*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>ring4(t) = (2*cos(t), 2*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.5,-2.4,2.7,2.2)">
<parametric-curve at="cy-ring1" function="ring1" domain="(0,2*pi)"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="cy-ring2" function="ring2" domain="(0,2*pi)"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="cy-ring3" function="ring3" domain="(0,2*pi)"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="cy-ring4" function="ring4" domain="(0,2*pi)"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<line at="cy-diag1" endpoints="((-1.55,-1.55),(1.55,1.55))"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<line at="cy-diag2" endpoints="((-1.55,1.55),(1.55,-1.55))"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<axes at="cy-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="cy-curve" function="card" domain="(0,2*pi)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<label at="cy-eq" anchor="(-2.4,1.35)" alignment="e"
    color="#0072B2"><m>r = 1-\sin(\theta)</m></label>
<label at="cy-r1" anchor="(0.5,0.06)" alignment="n">
<m>\tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="cy-r2" anchor="(1.02,0.06)" alignment="n">
<m>1</m>
</label>
<label at="cy-r4" anchor="(2.02,0.06)" alignment="n">
<m>2</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A polar grid with concentric gray circles. The cardioid r equals one minus sine theta is drawn in blue: a heart shaped curve pointing downward, widest at the bottom where theta equals three pi over two and r equals two, with a cusp at the origin at the top where theta equals pi over two. The curve is symmetric about the y axis.">
  <annotation ref="cy-curve" text="The cardioid r equals one minus sine theta, symmetric about the y axis."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-y-video">
<caption>
Animation of graphing <m>r = 1 - \sin\theta</m>. As the traced point
(in blue) sweeps out the cardioid, its mirror image across the
<m>y</m>-axis (in green) lands on the same curve, showing the symmetry
about the <m>y</m>-axis.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-cardioid-y-trace"
       source="videos/cardioid_y_trace_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-symmetry-origin">
<title>Symmetry about the origin</title>

<p>
If the point <m>(r,\theta)</m> is on the graph, then the point
<m>(-r,\theta)</m> or <m>(r,\theta+\pi)</m> lies on the graph as well.
As an example, <m>r^2 = \sin\theta</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-origin-symmetry-prefigure"/>, is symmetric about the
origin: its graph consists of the two branches
<m>r = \sqrt{\sin\theta}</m> and <m>r = -\sqrt{\sin\theta}</m>, and
replacing <m>r</m> by <m>-r</m> leaves the equation unchanged. The two
branches are traced simultaneously in
<xref ref="fig-origin-symmetry-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-origin-symmetry-prefigure">
<caption>
The graph of <m>r^2 = \sin\theta</m>, made of the branch
<m>r = \sqrt{\sin\theta}</m> (in blue) and the branch
<m>r = -\sqrt{\sin\theta}</m> (in vermillion). Since replacing
<m>r</m> by <m>-r</m> does not change the equation, the graph is
symmetric about the origin.
</caption>
<image width="65%">
<shortdescription>
Two petal-shaped loops symmetric through the origin on a polar grid.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-origin-symmetry">
<diagram dimensions="(360,328)" margins="10">
<definition>pos(t) = (sqrt(sin(t))*cos(t), sqrt(sin(t))*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>neg(t) = (-sqrt(sin(t))*cos(t), -sqrt(sin(t))*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>ring1(t) = (0.5*cos(t), 0.5*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>ring2(t) = (cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.45,1.7,1.45)">
<parametric-curve at="os-ring1" function="ring1" domain="(0,2*pi)"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="os-ring2" function="ring2" domain="(0,2*pi)"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<line at="os-diag1" endpoints="((-0.9,-0.9),(0.9,0.9))"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<line at="os-diag2" endpoints="((-0.9,0.9),(0.9,-0.9))"
    stroke="#dddddd" thickness="1"/>
<axes at="os-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="os-pos" function="pos" domain="(0.001,3.14)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="os-neg" function="neg" domain="(0.001,3.14)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="os-pos-label" anchor="(-1.45,0.8)" alignment="e"
    color="#0072B2"><m>r = \sqrt{\sin\theta}</m></label>
<label at="os-neg-label" anchor="(1.62,-0.85)" alignment="w"
    color="#D55E00"><m>r = -\sqrt{\sin\theta}</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A polar grid with two gray circles of radii one half and one. Two petal shaped loops: a blue loop above the x axis, the branch r equals the square root of sine theta, and a vermillion loop below the x axis, the branch r equals negative the square root of sine theta. Together they form a graph symmetric about the origin.">
  <annotation ref="os-pos" text="The branch r equals the positive square root of sine theta, a loop above the x axis."/>
  <annotation ref="os-neg" text="The branch r equals the negative square root of sine theta, a loop below the x axis, the reflection of the blue loop through the origin."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-origin-symmetry-video">
<caption>
Animation of graphing <m>r^2 = \sin\theta</m>. For each
<m>\theta \in [0,\pi]</m>, both <m>(r,\theta)</m> with
<m>r = \sqrt{\sin\theta}</m> (in blue) and <m>(-r,\theta)</m> (in
vermillion) satisfy the equation, so the two branches are traced
simultaneously and the graph is symmetric about the origin.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-origin-symmetry-trace"
       source="videos/origin_symmetry_trace_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</paragraphs>

</subsection>

</section>
